From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller: stop claiming a departed client is associated
Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>

sta.associated is written as (state != STA_DISCONNECTED), and only for the
stations a node currently reports. A client that roamed away is never written
again, so its row keeps associated=1 for as long as it exists. The
event-driven delete cannot help either: mapc_del_sta() runs only when a
disassoc event arrives whose BSSID and node both match the stored ones, and a
roaming client leaves without disassociating at all.

Measured on the live database 2026-08-04: 20 rows, every single one claiming
to be associated, against three real associations in the whole mesh. bpi-x8
alone held five while serving no client.

Two additions, and both only ever CLEAR a flag. Nothing is deleted, so sta
keeps the departed-client history it is meant to keep.

1. Per station, in mapc_sync_sta(). One MAC cannot be associated to two nodes
   at once, so the moment a node reports a client, rows for that client under
   a different node are stale by definition. This covers roaming, and it is
   the only one of the two that can reach a node which has lost ALL of its
   clients: such a node reports an empty stalist, and the sweep deliberately
   returns on that so a half-parsed CMDU cannot wipe the table.

2. Per node, in mapc_sync_sta_sweep(). Clients this node no longer reports
   are marked gone. The existing comment there is right that such rows must
   not be DELETED on a single snapshot, because the enumeration can
   transiently under-report. A flag is a different trade: an under-report
   costs one flip, and the very next Topology Response, which is where this
   runs, reports the client again and the upsert sets it back to 1.

Same guards as the existing sweep: keyed on agent_almac so only that node's
rows are touched, and an empty or absurdly wide report returns early.

--- a/src/db/mapc_sync.c	2026-08-04 18:39:08.388531026 +0200
+++ b/src/db/mapc_sync.c	2026-08-04 18:39:08.388579463 +0200
@@ -549,6 +549,31 @@
 			mapdb_bind_null(st, i);
 	}
 	stmt_finish(c->db, st);
+
+	/* 999992: one MAC cannot be associated to two nodes at once, so the
+	 * moment a node reports this client, every row for it under a DIFFERENT
+	 * node is stale by definition.
+	 *
+	 * This is the roaming case, and it is the one the snapshot sweep cannot
+	 * reach: a node that has lost ALL its clients reports an empty stalist,
+	 * and the sweep deliberately returns on that so a half-parsed CMDU
+	 * cannot wipe the table. Measured 2026-08-04: bpi-x8 held five rows all
+	 * claiming to be associated while serving no client at all.
+	 *
+	 * Safe without a snapshot: keyed on the client, driven by a positive
+	 * report, and it only ever clears a flag. */
+	{
+		static const char *elsewhere =
+			"UPDATE sta SET associated=0 "
+			"WHERE macaddr=? AND agent_almac<>? AND associated<>0";
+		mapdb_stmt *e = NULL;
+
+		if (mapdb_prepare(c->db, elsewhere, &e) == MAPDB_OK) {
+			mapdb_bind_text(e, 1, mac);
+			mapdb_bind_text(e, 2, almac);
+			stmt_finish(c->db, e);
+		}
+	}
 }
 
 void mapc_del_sta(struct controller *c, struct sta *s)
@@ -607,6 +632,48 @@
 		return;
 	}
 	mapdb_bind_text(st, 1, almac);
+	i = 2;
+	list_for_each_entry(s, &n->stalist, list) {
+		char m[18];
+		mac2str(s->macaddr, m);
+		mapdb_bind_text(st, i++, m);
+	}
+	stmt_finish(c->db, st);
+
+	/* 999992: the same reconciliation for ordinary clients - but as a flag,
+	 * not a delete.
+	 *
+	 * associated is written as (state != STA_DISCONNECTED) and only for the
+	 * stations a node currently reports, so a client that roamed away is
+	 * never written again and its row keeps associated=1 forever. A roaming
+	 * client leaves without disassociating, and mapc_del_sta() runs only
+	 * when the event's BSSID and node both match the stored ones, so that
+	 * path never fires either. Measured 2026-08-04: 20 rows, every single
+	 * one claiming to be associated, against three real associations in the
+	 * whole mesh.
+	 *
+	 * The comment above is right that these rows must not be DELETED on one
+	 * snapshot. A flag is a different trade: a node that transiently
+	 * under-reports costs one flip, and the very next Topology Response -
+	 * which is where this runs - reports the client again and the upsert
+	 * sets it back to 1. Nothing is lost, and sta keeps its history, which
+	 * is what it is for.
+	 *
+	 * Same guards as the delete above: keyed on agent_almac so only this
+	 * node's rows are touched, and an empty or absurdly wide report has
+	 * already returned. */
+	snprintf(sql, sizeof(sql),
+		 "UPDATE sta SET associated=0 WHERE COALESCE(is_bsta,0)=0 "
+		 "AND agent_almac=? AND associated<>0 AND macaddr NOT IN (");
+	for (i = 0; i < cnt; i++)
+		strcat(sql, i ? ",?" : "?");
+	strcat(sql, ")");
+
+	if (mapdb_prepare(c->db, sql, &st) != MAPDB_OK) {
+		warn("mapc_sync: sta depart prepare: %s\n", mapdb_last_error(c->db));
+		return;
+	}
+	mapdb_bind_text(st, 1, almac);
 	i = 2;
 	list_for_each_entry(s, &n->stalist, list) {
 		char m[18];
